sync - write out what the filesystem is holding back

SYNOPSIS
    sync

DESCRIPTION
    Changes to the tree are collected into a journal transaction and
    committed a little later, rather than one transaction per operation.
    Several operations in a row touch the same handful of metadata blocks
    - the same bitmap, the same directory, the same group descriptor - so
    a batch of them costs very little more than one of them did, and
    committing each separately roughly tripled the traffic to the disk.

    What that gives up is that a call which returned successfully is in
    the tree but not necessarily on the disk yet. sync is what makes it
    so, and returns when it is.

    A batch is also committed when it fills up, and when it is more than
    a second old and another request arrives.

    That last condition needs another request, which on an idle machine
    never comes: the server is asleep waiting for one and has no clock of
    its own to wake it. syncd runs from boot and calls this every five
    seconds so that idle is not the same as never - the same job, and the
    same interval, as update on early UNIX.

SEE ALSO
    fsck, mount, syncd

